[MUSIC]

00:00:09.833 --> 00:00:14.150
Hi, I'm Will Torres, and I'm joined by
my colleague Tiffany Barone Manalo.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:17.650
We're going to be talking about
Financial Aid, here at Stanford GSB, and

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:20.059
we're going to be going over some
frequently answered questions.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.810
&gt;&gt; In today's conversation, we're
going to talk about who should apply for

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:27.140
financial aid, when you should apply and
the types of aid available to you.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:30.506
&gt;&gt; After we go through the basics, we're
going to bring in some current student who

00:00:30.506 --> 00:00:34.127
went through the financial aid process,
and we'll be sharing their experiences.

00:00:34.127 --> 00:00:38.181
There are two terms to know,
need-blind and need-based.

00:00:38.181 --> 00:00:39.794
The admission process is need-blind,

00:00:39.794 --> 00:00:42.592
which means we're not taking into
account how you're going to pay for

00:00:42.592 --> 00:00:46.270
your education, when we're making
those admissions decisions.

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:48.750
The financial aid process
happens after you're admitted,

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.230
and financial aid here at
Stanford is need-based.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:55.130
That means you're going to be working
with our financial aid officers,

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:57.460
to take a look at your
financial circumstances,

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:01.310
to figure out a financial aid package that
will support you during your time here.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:07.550
The financial aid process, is centered
around our school's guiding principles

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:10.190
of access, equity and excellence.

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:14.590
We want every student who is admitted,
to be able to engage and

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:17.390
participate to the fullest
extent during their time here.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.500
To do this,
we've developed a financial aid process,

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:26.310
to treat students consistently and
equitably, while supporting

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:30.120
the GSB's efforts to attract the most
competitive and diverse class possible.

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:36.200
We understand that a Stanford degree is
an investment in you and your future.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.610
And we hope that with our
financial aid support,

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.610
you'll be able to make that investment,

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:43.770
with a goal of supporting those who
need it the most, with the most aid.

00:01:45.568 --> 00:01:48.160
&gt;&gt; So I'm going to provide you with
a general overview of financial aid at

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:49.490
Stanford GSB.

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:51.580
So all admitted students into the MBA and

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:55.600
MSS program, are eligible to apply for
need based fellowship and loans.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.770
Loans are specific based
off a year citizenship.

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:03.250
We determine your cost of attendance based
off a year unique financial situation so,

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:05.340
marital status, as well as family size.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.920
Your cost of attendance
takes into consideration,

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.850
your tuition, living expenses,
housing charges, health insurance, etc.

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:16.140
We determine your student contribution
based off of the information reported in

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:16.900
your application.

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:21.530
We take into consideration special
circumstances which include

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:24.848
medical expenses, support to family,
as well as your family size.

00:02:24.848 --> 00:02:29.902
On average, 50% of our students receive
GSB fellowship, which equates so that

00:02:29.902 --> 00:02:36.890
$76,000 for the two year MBA program, and
$50,000 for their one year MSx program.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:40.135
The GSB has an exception, that all
incidents will contribute a portion of

00:02:40.135 --> 00:02:42.660
their incoming assets towards
their educational cost.

00:02:45.133 --> 00:02:48.176
&gt;&gt; One of the most commonly
asked questions that I get is,

00:02:48.176 --> 00:02:51.800
how do you determine a student's
fellowship eligibility?

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:54.460
&gt;&gt; Yes, that is a typical question
that we received from prospective

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:55.480
admitted students.

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:58.797
So in order to determine somebody's
need-based fellowship eligibility, we're

00:02:58.797 --> 00:03:02.067
going to look at their income and assets
to determine their student contribution,

00:03:02.067 --> 00:03:04.981
which is what they should be able to
contribute towards their education.

00:03:04.981 --> 00:03:07.670
We take that student contribution
plus a base loan and

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:12.051
deduct it from their costs of attendance,
to determine what their financial need is.

00:03:12.051 --> 00:03:13.252
Of that financial need,

00:03:13.252 --> 00:03:17.252
we're going to give as much as we can in
need -based fellowship, and the rest would

00:03:17.252 --> 00:03:20.753
come in the form of loans which
are specific based off your citizenship.

00:03:20.753 --> 00:03:25.472
And just coming from lived experience,
life is complicated.

00:03:25.472 --> 00:03:28.168
So how do you take in to account personal,

00:03:28.168 --> 00:03:31.720
and/or like unique
circumstances from applicants?

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:33.100
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a great question.

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:37.142
So within our financial application,
we have a section specifically for

00:03:37.142 --> 00:03:41.445
students to call out unique financial
situations and provide us contact, and

00:03:41.445 --> 00:03:45.642
we will review them and take them into
consideration on a case by case basis.

00:03:45.642 --> 00:03:50.382
&gt;&gt; And then what about folks who
are thinking about non-profit or

00:03:50.382 --> 00:03:56.208
public sector areas, that historically
might have a different [INAUDIBLE]?

00:03:56.208 --> 00:03:59.007
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So,
what's really amazing is that the GSB

00:03:59.007 --> 00:04:02.832
offers a loan forgiveness program for
both the MBA and MSx alumni,

00:04:02.832 --> 00:04:06.810
who are working in a non-profit
public service sector.

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:12.470
&gt;&gt; Great, and how do you consider folks
coming from outside the United States,

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:15.310
so international versus US citizens?

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:17.770
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
So for us, for financial aid purposes,

00:04:17.770 --> 00:04:21.120
we treat both internationals and US
citizens and permanent residents exactly

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:25.165
the same in our financial aid algorithm,
to determine need based aid eligibility.

00:04:25.165 --> 00:04:27.040
So,whatever one student will get,

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.850
would be the same that somebody
else would get in the calculation.

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:35.320
&gt;&gt; Great, and then what if I'm coming
to the GSB with a spouse or children?

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:36.920
How do you take that into account?

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:39.410
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so if you're coming
